# Configuration for deploying TravelAgentWebStack (the hosted web UI on
# ECS Fargate behind a plain, non-authenticating ALB — the server itself
# runs the OIDC login flow against Okta; see DESIGN.md §2e, PLAN.md
# Phase 12). Optional: `cdk deploy`/`cdk synth` skip WebStack entirely if
# WEB_CERTIFICATE_ARN is unset, so you don't need this file at all if
# you're only running web/server.py locally for development.
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values below. cdk/app.py loads it
# via a small dotenv-style reader (_load_dotenv()).
#
# Register a new, dedicated Okta OIDC web application (confidential
# client, with a client secret) for this purpose — do not reuse any other
# Okta app in your org for this. Configure it for the `openid
# offline_access` scopes and PKCE (S256), and configure it to keep
# issuing the *same*, non-rotating refresh token on every refresh (not
# Okta's rotate-on-use alternative) — this project's silent-refresh logic
# assumes a fixed refresh token. Register
# https://<WEB_HOSTNAME below>/oauth2/callback as an allowed redirect URI.

# The friendly domain you (or your organization) have pointed at this
# stack's ALB — DNS is explicitly out of scope for this stack (see
# README's "Hosting the Web UI"), so this must be a domain you manage
# yourself, already pointed (e.g. via CNAME/A record) at the ALB's DNS
# name (available from the AlbDnsName stack output after the first
# deploy). This exact hostname is used to build the OIDC redirect_uri —
# it must match both the DNS record you've created and the redirect URI
# registered on the Okta app below, or login fails with a redirect_uri
# mismatch error from Okta.
WEB_HOSTNAME=travel-agent.example.com

# ARN of an existing ACM certificate for the ALB's HTTPS listener. Must
# already be issued and validated in the same region as the deployment
# (us-east-1) and must cover WEB_HOSTNAME above. This project does not
# provision or import a certificate for you.
WEB_CERTIFICATE_ARN=arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:123456789012:certificate/xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Okta OIDC app endpoints and credentials for web/server.py's own OIDC
# flow. Find these in your Okta org's /.well-known/openid-configuration
# document for the authorization server this app is registered under.
# The client secret is stored in a Secrets Manager secret this stack
# provisions (OidcClientSecret) — it is never passed to the container as
# a plain environment variable.
WEB_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/default
WEB_OIDC_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/default/v1/authorize
WEB_OIDC_TOKEN_ENDPOINT=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/default/v1/token
WEB_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=0oaXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
WEB_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-here

# Phase 2 auth rearchitecture: a second, dedicated Okta app used to
# exchange a logged-in user's Okta access token (from the app above) for
# a Runtime-audienced JWT via RFC 8693 OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange — the web
# server presents this exchanged token to AgentCore's InvokeAgentRuntime
# instead of signing the call with IAM/SigV4. See DESIGN.md's Phase 2
# section for the full design.
#
# This must be a real Okta "API Services" app (not the confidential-
# client OIDC app above) with the "Token Exchange" grant type enabled
# (Applications > [this app] > General > Grant type > Advanced > Token
# Exchange). It can use the same or a different Okta authorization server
# as the app above — if different, configure "Trusted servers" between
# the two authorization servers in the Okta Admin Console (Security > API
# > [this app's auth server] > Trusted servers) so the exchange can
# validate a subject token issued by the *other* server.
#
# WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_AUDIENCE and WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_SCOPE are arbitrary
# strings you choose when setting up the exchange app's custom scope and
# access policy — they must exactly match the allowed_audience/
# allowed_scopes configured on the Runtime's own JWT authorizer (see
# cdk/stacks/runtime_stack.py and this same WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_AUDIENCE/
# WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_SCOPE, which this CDK app also reads directly to
# configure that authorizer — see cdk/app.py). Do not include the
# `openid`/`offline_access` scopes here — Okta does not support them for
# a service-app-initiated token exchange (no ID token or refresh token is
# issued from this grant).
#
# The client secret is stored in a separate Secrets Manager secret this
# stack provisions (RuntimeOidcClientSecret) — it is never passed to the
# container as a plain environment variable.
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/your-runtime-auth-server-id
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_TOKEN_ENDPOINT=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/your-runtime-auth-server-id/v1/token
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=0oaXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=your-runtime-exchange-client-secret-here
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_AUDIENCE=travel-agent-runtime
WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_SCOPE=runtime:invoke

# Phase 3 (Gateway/OBO auth rearchitecture): a third, separate Okta app
# used by AgentCore Runtime to exchange the Runtime-audienced JWT above
# (received as this Runtime's own inbound token) for a Gateway-audienced
# JWT via RFC 8693 On-Behalf-Of token exchange — the Runtime presents
# this exchanged token to the Gateway's MCP endpoint instead of
# SigV4-signing the call. Unlike Phase 2's exchange (performed by
# web/server.py directly), this exchange is performed entirely by AWS's
# own AgentCore Identity service (GetResourceOauth2Token) — agent.py never
# makes an HTTP call to Okta itself. See DESIGN.md's Phase 3 section for
# the full design.
#
# Same "API Services" app type as the WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_* app above (Token
# Exchange grant type, DPoP disabled, real client_id claim on issued
# tokens) — but this is a genuinely separate, third Okta app, not a reuse
# of either of the first two. GATEWAY_OIDC_AUDIENCE/GATEWAY_OIDC_SCOPE
# follow the same convention as WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_AUDIENCE/
# WEB_RUNTIME_OIDC_SCOPE: arbitrary strings you choose that must exactly
# match the allowed_audience/allowed_scopes configured on the Gateway's
# own JWT authorizer (see cdk/stacks/gateway_stack.py and cdk/app.py,
# which reads this same GATEWAY_OIDC_AUDIENCE/GATEWAY_OIDC_SCOPE to
# configure that authorizer directly).
#
# The client secret is passed to GatewayStack's CfnOAuth2CredentialProvider
# as a plain literal (via SecretValue.unsafe_plain_text()) — AWS's live
# CreateOauth2CredentialProvider API rejects a Secrets-Manager-reference
# (clientSecretConfig) for CLIENT_SECRET_BASIC/CLIENT_SECRET_POST auth
# regardless of explicit clientAuthenticationMethod (confirmed live; see
# DESIGN.md decision #90). AgentCore Identity itself still stores this
# secret server-side (in its own token vault, not visible to this
# project's code) and reads it during the exchange — the Runtime's
# execution role never touches Secrets Manager directly for this (see
# runtime_stack.py's module docstring).
#
# Unlike the two WEB_* blocks above, GatewayStack reads these directly —
# they are NOT part of TravelAgentWebStack's required-together WEB_* set,
# and can be configured independently of whether WebStack is deployed.
GATEWAY_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-org.okta.com/oauth2/your-gateway-auth-server-id
GATEWAY_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=0oaXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
GATEWAY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=your-gateway-obo-client-secret-here
GATEWAY_OIDC_AUDIENCE=travel-agent-gateway
GATEWAY_OIDC_SCOPE=gateway:invoke
